  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Knowing about Genocide is an open-access scholarly book with an early but visible uptake: it has 2 citations in OpenAlex, one recorded teaching-related video discussion, and library holdings that indicate discoverability in academic collections. Its strongest reach is in sociology and adjacent social-science conversations about genocide, denial, and human rights.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Joachim J. Savelsberg\u2019s Knowing about Genocide examines how conflicting knowledge about the Armenian genocide is produced and sustained, making it a focused contribution to sociology, criminology, and political inquiry into denial, disinformation, and human rights. The book\u2019s open-access status likely supports its circulation, and the record shows at least one recorded educational discussion alongside library holdings, suggesting use as a teaching and reference text.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is still early, with 2 citations recorded in OpenAlex. Those citations appear in journal articles published in 2021 and 2022, including venues in sociology, which is consistent with the book\u2019s disciplinary framing. The citation record is too small to support any claims about broader field standing, but it does show that the book has begun to enter ongoing academic discussion.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The thematic profile centers on genocide, denial, Armenian history, and disinformation, with strong alignment to Peace, Justice and strong institutions. That alignment underscores the book\u2019s relevance beyond a single case study: it addresses how societies contest historical violence and how acknowledgment, silencing, and political power shape public knowledge.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the record depicts a work with clear pedagogical and policy relevance and an emerging scholarly footprint. The evidence is modest but coherent: the book is visible in academic collections, has been discussed in an educational video setting, and is already being cited in sociology-oriented research.</p>",
